  "body": {
    "id": "prop:point-identification",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If \\(\\mathcal C_P\\neq\\varnothing\\), then WAOSS is point identified if and only if \\(\\Theta(P)\\) is a singleton, if and only if \\(V_P^+=V_P^-\\), if and only if \\(R(P)=0\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mathcal C_P",
      "\\Theta(P)",
      "V_P^+",
      "V_P^-",
      "R(P)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:attained-interval",
      "def:frontier-values"
    ],
    "justification": "This corollary records the exact singleton condition implied by the attained endpoint formula and is not presented as an independent diagnostic or novelty claim.",
    "gap": "The published conditional-support results give sufficient singleton regimes; this algebraic corollary only records when the new sharp interval collapses.",
    "consumer": "Researchers can report whether the computed sharp obstacle interval is a singleton without attributing additional identifying content to a reparameterized width condition.",
    "proof_tex": "By the attained-interval theorem and \\(A>0\\), the nonempty set \\(\\Theta(P)\\) is the closed interval\n\\[\n\\Theta(P)=\n\\left[\\frac{T-V_P^+}{A},\\frac{T-V_P^-}{A}\\right].\n\\]\nIts length is\n\\[\n\\frac{T-V_P^-}{A}-\\frac{T-V_P^+}{A}\n=\\frac{V_P^+-V_P^-}{A}\n=\\frac{R(P)}{A},\n\\]\nwhere the final equality is the definition of \\(R(P)\\).  A nonempty closed interval is a singleton if and only if its length is zero.  Because \\(A>0\\), this occurs if and only if \\(V_P^+=V_P^-\\), equivalently if and only if \\(R(P)=0\\).  Full-law sharpness identifies \\(\\Theta(P)\\) with the set of causal values generated by legal completions, so this singleton condition is exactly point identification of WAOSS rather than merely uniqueness of an unrelated optimization functional.",
